age  you learn the bigger the challenge and  the more interesting you can get as well  right it's a bit like if you go for a  walk a hike in the woods  and your only concern is to reach the  finish line  you're not going to enjoy the forest  indeed  so you go on a shorter walk a longer  walk spanish is a shorter walk  chinese is a longer walk but if you  enjoy the process it doesn't matter but  you should be realistic as to the amount  of time it takes  okay on to my third question you you  talked about technology and how language  learning has become easier through  things such as mp3s and ipods and and  whatever ipads or whatever and what's  your opinion an experience of classroom  based language teaching and do you think  the way languages are taught in school  should be reformed you think that for  example they could make use better use  of technology  well let me quote a few  sort of facts i read once  on the internet that in new brunswick  which is the only bilingual province in  canada as you know  uh in the  french in the english language school  system  they teach french from grade 1  and they teach 30 minutes a day of  french  and they found that after 12 years  the average level of oral proficiency of  the graduates of the new brunswick  english language school system  the the  the number of students who achieved what  they called intermediate oral  proficiency  was 0.66  wow  wow i imagine that there are more people  than that who have a francophone parent  in other words the school system was  essentially irrelevant  in terms of the goal and i think if you  look at the english language school  system most  kids if they're not in french immersion  after six eight depending on the  province however many years they  graduate and they're not capable of  speaking french  and i gather that the same is true in  the us as far as spanish instruction so  that's at one end of the spectrum on the  other hand i attended a meeting of the  american uh  whatever it's called a c  association of teachers of foreign  language and i met some excellent people  down there i met one lady chinese lady  in her late 50s who was using the iphone  in her classroom  to help teach  chinese characters so i am sure that  there are many innovative teachers in  the system who are using technology  my over my reaction overall  has been that the educational  establishment  language teachers in our schools or for  that matter for that matter uh the whole  esl program for immigrants  that the  major motivation of the practitioners  the teachers there is to protect the  existing system  and to protect the dominant position of  the classroom  to protect the idea that it's the  teacher who says the curriculum the  teacher decides what people are going to  do and they're  largely reluctant  and even those teachers who want to  wander away from that are prevented from  doing so by  the system by  the bureaucracy by the language school  director so and maybe for the parents  who who want  the conventional they want to see their  kids tested on something  so you know in the case of french  instruction in english canadian schools  kids are tested and some kids do well  because in school some kids do well in  math and some kids do poorly and they'll  be tested as i'm sure in new brunswick  i'm sure the brighter kids did well on  the french test year after year after  year and at the end of 12 years they  couldn't speak french so so i think  there's some fundamentally  things that we have backwards in  language instruction one of the things  that i think is wrong  is is trying to get people to produce  the language too early  and then correcting them all the time  i don't think it's helpful  i think they're far  it's far better to focus on  understanding and finding ways to allow  the kids to enjoy the language and they  get it wrong they get it wrong what does  it matter that  so and so student in new brunswick  either did well or didn't do well in  these tests on you know  the subjunctive or conjugating verbs if  at the end of the 12 years it can't  speak anyway  it doesn't matter it doesn't matter so  yeah i think there's some real issues uh  but i think more and more  uh  even in the classroom if the mo if the  learner isn't motivated he's not he or  she's not gonna learn so so no i think  what's wrong is that they focus on  getting people used to language too  early  i think you need a lot of understanding  you need to focus on comprehension  understanding which can be done for kids  you know they can watch cartoons movies  find books for them to read uh things to  listen to and don't force them to try  and speak until they until they want to  but at first parents don't want that  they want to see the little kid say  something in french then there because  the little  you know whatever can say something so i  i hope that that changes  okay very good i'm hopeful  i'm not hosting insights  yeah  okay steve well before we finish off  this interview um do you have any  additional tips to language learners or  maybe um some some suggestions that you  would like to make to people  well i think language learning is is  very much a personal journey  and so  and the the uh  the number one issue is motivation  so if people really want to learn a  language then they have to take charge  they have to find  the method of learning that they like  the best  because if they like that method of  learning they will put in enough time  and ultimately  in order to succeed in language learning  you have to put in the time  you're gonna you're only gonna put in  the time if you're motivated if you find  different ways of learning that you like  you know  to me is listening and reading i don't  do much flashcarding i don't you know  don't like i'm not that concerned about  speaking until i have a certain  foundation in the language this is a  method that's worked for me but it might  very well be that other people have no  interest in reading no interest in  leading in listening so they have to  find their own path  but it's important to find the path that  works for you so that you enjoy it so  you put in the time because if you enjoy  it and you put in the time you will  succeed  well thank you so much steve these were  very interesting insights and i hope  people are going to have enjoyed this  interview thanks for your time  it was fantastic speaking to you okay  thank you  you
 

